Thought: Both SMILES strings are valid. To determine which isomer matches the compound in question, I should compare their NMR spectra. I will start by obtaining the proton NMR spectra for the first valid SMILES string.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1=CC=C2C(=C1)[C@H](OC2=O)C3=CC=NC=C3"}

Thought: The search results from the NMRShift database did not provide a direct match for the SMILES string "C1=CC=C2C(=C1)[C@H](OC2=O)C3=CC=NC=C3". I will now proceed to validate and analyze the next SMILES string to see if it provides a better match or more relevant data.
Action: search_by_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1=CC=C(C(=C1)C2=CC(=C(C=C2)C#N)O)O"}

Thought: The search results for the second SMILES string also did not provide a direct match or relevant data for the compound. Given the lack of direct matches from the NMRShift database, I should consider using the NMR spectra tools to directly measure the spectra of the sample at hand. This will help in comparing the experimental data with the proposed structures. I will start with the proton NMR spectra.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The proton NMR spectra provides specific chemical shifts and coupling constants that can be used to identify the structure of the compound. The presence of a doublet at 1.43 ppm, a singlet at 2.69 ppm, a quartet at 5.05 ppm, and aromatic signals between 7.13 and 7.90 ppm suggest a structure with an aromatic ring and possibly an ester or similar functional group. I will now obtain the carbon NMR spectra to gather more information about the carbon environments in the compound.
